        "story": "As we drive down Fifth Street, Cliff keeps fiddling with the gears, radio, and AC in complete overkill, like he's operating an airplane. But I can't blame him for being nervous; last time we took this drive, our faces ended up smooshed together. \"So,\" Cliff begins with a huff, breaking the silence, \"the experts claim that letting someone go on a Monday leads to fewer incidents.\" \"It's good you did it today, then.\" A tiny chortle escapes from us both at the same time. Our eyes shift together, our equilibrium teasingly close to returning. \"Wanna know something fucked up?\" Cliff says, half smiling. \"After letting someone go, I pretend they died. That's easier to deal with for some reason.\" \"That's really sweet, Cliff.\" And I pull his thermos from the cup holder in the console and hold it forward. \"To Larry, may he rest in peace.\" This time he allows himself to fully grin.",
        "story_cn": "沿第五大道行驶时，克里夫不断摆弄换挡杆、收音机和空调，操作过度得像在驾驶飞机。但我没法怪他紧张——上次我们走这条路，最后可是在彼此脸上留下了压痕。“所以说啊，”克里夫呼着气打破沉默，“专家声称周一解雇员工能减少冲突事件。”“那你今天做得挺对。”两人同时漏出短促的轻笑，视线相触时，那种微妙的平衡感又戏谑地回到了我们之间。“想听件混账事吗？”克里夫半笑着说，“每次解雇别人后，我都假装他们死了。不知为何这样更容易承受。”“真是贴心呢，克里夫。”我从中央扶手的杯架抽出他的保温杯举起来，“敬拉里，愿他安息。”这次他终于放任自己咧开嘴笑了。",
